# Panel transitions

## Purpose

Control what happens between panels. The reader builds the story in the
gutter, so the plan states what each gutter asks them to supply and draws
everything they could not supply on their own.

## Scope

Layout layer. Applies to the `moment`, `transition_in`, and `ellipsis` fields
of every panel in `page.yaml`, and to the ledger rows on both sides of a
gutter. Used by `/page-plan`, `/panel-script`, `/panel-ledger`, and
`/smart-page`. The ledger itself is governed by `visual/state-continuity.md`
and `visual/spatial-continuity.md`.

## Core Principles

- One panel, one drawable moment. A panel is a single instant that an artist
  could draw without asking "which part?".
- Closure is a budget. Each gutter asks the reader to infer something, and
  the plan knows what.
- An omission is either inferable or labelled. Anything else is a gap.
- A caption never repairs an impossible drawing. If the pictures do not show
  how a character got somewhere or who holds an object, add a panel.

## The six transition types

`transition_in` names how a panel follows the one before it. It is `null`
only for the first panel of a scene.

| Value | The gutter moves between | Typical use |
|---|---|---|
| `moment` | Two instants of the same action, a breath apart | Slowing time: a hand closing, a look held |
| `action` | Two stages of one subject's action | Most physical storytelling: reach, then grab |
| `subject` | Two subjects inside the same scene and time | Dialogue, reaction, cutting from actor to target |
| `scene` | Two places or two times | Scene changes and time jumps |
| `aspect` | Two views of the same place and mood, with time suspended | Atmosphere: the lamp, the river, the wet deck |
| `non_sequitur` | Two images with no narrative link | Dream logic and deliberate dissonance |

- `action` and `subject` carry most pages. A page with neither SHOULD be a
  deliberate mood or montage page, and its `purpose` says so.
- `moment` transitions cost space for very little story time. Default: runs
  of 2 to 4 panels, used where the beat deserves the slowdown.
- A `scene` transition needs an anchor in the arriving panel: an
  establishing view, a caption with time or place, or a landmark from the
  new location's map.
- `aspect` panels need no cast, and off-panel characters keep their ledger
  rows across them.
- `non_sequitur` requires a reason in the panel's `notes`. Without one it is
  treated as a mislabelled transition.

## One drawable main moment

- `moment` describes one instant in the present tense. If the sentence needs
  "then", "and afterwards", or two verbs performed in sequence by the same
  character, split the panel.
- Simultaneous supporting action goes in the background only when it stays
  legible at the panel's size. The main moment always wins the foreground.
- The actor of the main moment MUST be unmistakable from the drawing:
  framing, staging, and the cast row's `action` agree on who does what.
- A panel whose only content is a caption explaining what happened is a
  missing panel. Draw the event or cut it.

## Closure and what the gutter may skip

The gutter may skip what any reader would fill in the same way:

- travel across a single level of a location, when the start and the end
  are both shown;
- the middle of a routine action (a door opened, a coat put down), when the
  result is visible;
- time between scenes, when the arriving panel is anchored;
- the completed arc of an action whose start and consequence are shown.

The gutter MUST NOT hide:

- a custody change that a later panel depends on. Handoffs, pickups, and
  drops are drawn, and the panel that shows one carries the `transfers`
  entry;
- a change of level (deck to wheelhouse) when the character acts from the
  new level in the next panel, or when the location map has no `connections`
  entry that makes the route obvious;
- the first appearance of an injury or other state tag that later panels
  rely on;
- the identity of whoever performs a decisive action. If the reader has to
  guess who fired, the panel failed.

## Labelled ellipses

- When a panel follows a deliberate omission, `ellipsis` states what was
  skipped in plain words ("Mara climbs down the ladder"). `null` means the
  gutter skips nothing a reader would need to know about.
- An ellipsis is honest about consequences. If the skipped action changes a
  position, the cast row also sets `moved`. If it changes custody, the
  `transfers` entry is still recorded on a panel.
- Omit only what the reader can infer without confusing identity, position,
  or causality. If a test reader of the thumbnails asks "how did she get
  there?", the ellipsis failed and the action is drawn.
- Do not stack omissions. Two labelled ellipses in a row around the same
  character SHOULD become one drawn panel.

## Validation

- `schemas/page.schema.json` restricts `transition_in` to the six values or
  `null` and requires `moment` on every panel.
- `scripts/check-continuity.js` reports a character whose `position.level`
  changed since their previous appearance in the scene with neither `moved`
  on the row nor `ellipsis` on the panel, a prop whose holder changed with
  no `transfers` entry, and a state tag that vanished without a
  `state_changes.resolves` entry.
- `hooks/detect-continuity-drift.js` runs the same audit for the issue after
  a `page.yaml` write and warns about the findings on that page.
- Whether `moment` is one drawable instant, and whether an ellipsis is
  inferable, cannot be computed from the file. The script-editor and the
  layout-artist judge both in the self-review of `/panel-script` and on the
  thumbnails.
- In the rendered page, the `movement`, `object_custody`, and
  `role_causality` checks of the visual review confirm that the pixels show
  what the gutters were not allowed to hide.
